<h2>The prosecution case</h2>
<p>Manoj Kumar Jha died of a stab wound to the chest on 31 January 2021 at a vacant plot in Uttam Nagar. The previous day, the deceased and a witness had objected to the appellant and a juvenile (the CCL) consuming drugs at the same plot — the prosecution's motive. No one saw the stabbing. CCTV footage showed two boys entering the plot and running out; two witnesses identified the appellant as one of them. A buttondar knife, medically capable of causing the injuries, was recovered — but from the personal search of the CCL, not the appellant. Both had burnt their upper clothes, and the remnants were recovered at their instance.</p>

<h2>Where the trial court went wrong</h2>
<p>The trial court convicted the appellant under Sections 302/201/34 IPC and imposed life imprisonment. The Division Bench accepted the circumstantial foundation — presence at the plot, flight, false conduct afterwards — but drew the line at murder. Nothing on record showed who inflicted the fatal blow, and no overt act was attributed to the appellant. To stretch Section 34 across that gap would convert companionship into complicity in killing.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Finding</th><th>Consequence</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Presence at the scene with the CCL, established by CCTV and identification</td><td>Participation in the joint enterprise proved</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Knife carried by, and recovered from, the CCL alone</td><td>Fatal act not attributable to the appellant</td></tr>
  <tr><td>No evidence of a shared design to kill</td><td>Section 302/34 conviction unsustainable</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Knowing accompaniment of an armed companion after the previous day's quarrel</td><td>Conviction altered to Section 326/34 — grievous hurt by dangerous weapon</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Burning of clothes, recovery on disclosure</td><td>Section 201/34 conviction and three-year sentence upheld</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The sentencing outcome</h2>
<p>Having altered the principal conviction, the Bench sentenced the appellant under Section 326/34 to the period already undergone — recorded at approximately five years and eight months — maintained the three-year term under Section 201/34, and directed release forthwith unless required in another case. The approach reflects a consistent appellate pattern: where the proven offence is less grave than the charged offence, the time served during a long trial and appeal frequently absorbs the appropriate sentence.</p>

<h2>Why the judgment matters</h2>
<p>Group-presence cases built on CCTV are increasingly common in Delhi. This judgment is a careful reminder that the camera proves presence and flight, not intention. For the defence, the pressure points are attribution — who held the weapon, who struck — and the absence of any overt act. For the prosecution, the lesson is the converse: where two or more accused are caught on film, the case for murder against each of them still needs role-specific evidence, whether from eyewitnesses, forensics linking the weapon, or circumstances showing a common design to kill rather than to assault. The vicarious reach of Section 34 follows the intention actually shared, and the benefit of the ambiguity goes to the accused.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> In appeals against Section 302/34 convictions resting on circumstantial evidence, a structured attack works best: isolate each circumstance, test whether it proves presence or participation, and then ask what offence the participation — shorn of assumption — actually establishes. Alteration to a lesser offence with sentence undergone is often the realistic objective.</p>
</div>
